.TH SECSTORED 8
.SH NAME
secstored, secstore-setup \- secure store server, and its accounts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth/secstored
[
.B -d
] [
.BI -s " storedir"
] [
.BI -a " addr"
]
.PP
.B auth/secstore-setup
[
.B -f
] [
.BI -k " password"
] [
.BI -s " storedir"
] [
.BI -u " user"
] [
.BI -V " version"
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Secstored
serves the files of
.IR secstore (1)
and
.IR secstore (2):
for each user a few files, encrypted by the client, that it will give
to, and take from, whoever proves the user's password.
The profile starts one for each host user, on the loopback, and
.IR logon (1)
and
.IR factotum (4)
keep the user's keys in it, in the file
.BR factotum .
.I Addr
is the address announced (default
.BR tcp!127.0.0.1!5356 ),
.I storedir
the store (default
.BR /usr/inferno/secstore ),
and
.B -d
logs each request.
.PP
The store has a directory for each account, which holds
.TP 12n
.B PAK
the account's verifier, a line
.RI `` "version verifier" '':
what the server needs to check a password without being given it
(the PAK exchange of Plan 9's
.IR secstore ).
It is the server's own: no request fetches, stores or removes it under that name.
.TP
.B VAULTKEY
the hash of the account's vault key, when a second factor is enrolled (below).
.TP
.B 2fa
the client's key slots, when the client is on this machine; not served.
.TP
.I files
the account's files, as the client encrypted them.
A name is one name of a directory with no blank or control character in it;
names beginning with a dot are the server's while it writes.
.PP
A file is written whole beside the one it replaces and renamed over it.
.SS "Versions
The version is the first line of the exchange.
.B secstore4
is the current one.
Its verifier is made from the key the account's files are encrypted under
(100000 HMAC-SHA-256 of the password with the user's name;
.B mkseckey4
in
.IR secstore (2)),
so that a password tried against a stolen
.B PAK
costs what it costs against the stolen files beside it.
In a
.B secstore4
session a store or a remove is answered
.B OK
or with an error.
.PP
.B secstore3
(the same group and hash, the verifier made from one SHA-256 of the password)
and
.B secstore
(Plan 9's: a 1024-bit group and SHA-1)
are kept so that an account made under them can log in once more.
A session of either may read the account's files and give the account its
.B secstore4
verifier, which the client does; it does not store or remove, and is ended if it tries.
The new verifier is kept beside the old one until a
.B secstore4
login has worked, and the old one is then removed.
Whoever read the store of an account still at
.B secstore3
could try a password for one exponentiation; of one at
.BR secstore4 ,
for 100000 HMACs and the exponentiation.
.SS "Requests
After the exchange the session is encrypted
.RB ( aes_256_gcm ,
see
.IR ssl (3)),
and the client sends requests, a message each:
.TP
.BI GET " name"
The answer is the file's size and then its bytes;
.B -1
if the account has no such file; or an error, if it has and the file cannot be read
(it was
.B -1
for any file that could not be opened, which a client took for an account with nothing stored).
.B "GET ."
is a listing: for each file a line of its name, a tab, its size, its date (24 characters)
and the SHA-256 of its contents in base 64.
.TP
.BI PUT " name"
followed by the size and the bytes: store the file.
.TP
.BI RM " name"
Remove it.
.TP
.BI VERIFIER " secstore4 verifier"
Replace the account's verifier: from a session of an older version, the move to version 4;
from a
.B secstore4
session, a new password.
.TP
.B VAULT?
Answered
.B enrolled
or
.BR none .
.TP
.BI VAULTKEY " key"
Show the account's vault key (64 hexadecimal digits): answered
.BR OK ,
.B none
if no second factor is enrolled, or an error if it is not the key.
.TP
.BI ENROLL2FA " key"
Enrol a second factor: the server keeps the SHA-256 of
.IR key .
Refused if one is enrolled and its key has not been shown in this session.
.TP
.B UNENROLL2FA
Remove the enrolment; the key must have been shown.
.TP
.B BYE
End the session.
.SS "Second factor
An account whose files are under a data key that a second factor unwraps
(see
.B /docs/second-factor-auth.md
and
.IR twofaslot (2))
is
.I enrolled
at the server with a vault key made from the data key.
From then on the server stores and removes that account's files only in a session that has shown the vault key:
the password alone, which is all the exchange proves, reads the files (they are under the data key)
and changes nothing.
(Before this, whoever had the password could replace the vault with one of their own making,
under the password, which the login screen then loaded with no second factor.)
The states of an account are therefore: no
.B VAULTKEY
(the password alone changes its files), and enrolled.
.SS "Throttle
Ten logins in a row that do not prove an account's password lock the account for a minute
(NIST SP 800-53 AC-7); a wrong vault key is recorded in the audit log.
.SS "Accounts
.I Secstore-setup
makes an account for
.I user
(default: the current user) in
.IR storedir :
its directory and its
.BR PAK .
It asks for the password twice, or takes it from
.B -k
(which other programs can read in
.BR /prog :
for tests, and it says so).
The password must be at least 8 characters (NIST SP 800-63B 5.1.1.2:
the store is guessed at offline by whoever takes the disk).
An account that exists is not written over unless
.B -f
is given; its files stay under the old password.
.B -V
makes the verifier of an older
.I version
.RB ( secstore
or
.BR secstore3 ),
for tests of the move.
.IR Logon (1)
and
.IR factotum (4)
make an account the same way at a first login.
.SH FILES
.TF /usr/inferno/secstore/user/VAULTKEY
.TP
.BI /usr/inferno/secstore/ user /PAK
.TP
.BI /usr/inferno/secstore/ user /VAULTKEY
.TP
.BI /usr/inferno/secstore/ user /factotum
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/auth/secstored.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/auth/secstore-setup.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR logon (1),
.IR secstore (1),
.IR secstore (2),
.IR factotum (4)
.SH BUGS
A non-loopback listener lets anybody who reaches it ask whether an account exists
and guess at passwords, at the throttle's rate;
.I secstored
says so when started on one.
